<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/1st-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Beth Horon (66)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/66">66 CE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-jewish-roman-war">First Jewish–Roman War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Beth Horon was fought in the autumn of 66 CE, when Jewish rebels ambushed the retreating army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cestius-gallus">Cestius Gallus</a>, the Roman governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, in the Beth Horon pass north-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josephus">Josephus</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> lost nearly 6,000 men, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legio-xii-fulminata">Legio XII Fulminata</a> lost its eagle. The victory encouraged the revolt and led to the formation of a Jewish provisional government in Jerusalem.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-beth-horon-66.jpg" alt="Battle of Beth Horon (66)" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The original uploader was מיכאלי at Hebrew Wikipedia. · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Beit_Horon.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-jewish-roman-war">First Jewish–Roman War</a> began in the spring of 66 CE. A land dispute in the mixed city of Caesarea, in which the Roman authorities sided with the Greek community, was followed by the procurator <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gessius-florus">Gessius Florus</a> seizing 17 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talent">talents</a> from the Temple treasury and crushing the protests with killings and crucifixions. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agrippa-ii">Herod Agrippa II</a> failed to calm <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> and fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a> to inform <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cestius-gallus">Cestius Gallus</a>. Eleazar ben Hanania halted the daily sacrifices for the emperor, the Sicarii seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masada">Masada</a>, and in mid-September the last Roman garrisons in Jerusalem surrendered and were killed.</p><p>Gallus assembled an army of between 29,000 and 36,000 men around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legio-xii-fulminata">Legio XII Fulminata</a>, with detachments of the other three Syrian legions, auxiliary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cohort">cohorts</a> and cavalry, and troops of client kings such as Antiochus IV of Commagene and Sohaemus of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homs">Emesa</a>. He marched by way of Akko-Ptolemais, burned Chabulon and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaffa">Jaffa</a>, razed Lydda, reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> and burned parts of it, then lifted the siege for reasons that remain unclear.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>From his camp on Mount Scopus Gallus began to retreat. Jewish forces harried the column from the rear and both flanks, killing senior officers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/priscus">Priscus</a>, commander of the Sixth Legion, and forcing it to abandon most of its baggage. After two days' rest at Gabaon, near modern Al-Jib, Gallus ordered the pack animals killed, keeping only those needed for weapons and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siege-engine">siege engines</a>, and marched on towards the coastal plain.</p><p>In the narrow pass of Beth Horon, where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maccabees">Maccabees</a> had defeated a Seleucid army two centuries earlier, rebels on the cliffs above met the column with arrows and missiles. Rebel commanders included Simon Bar Giora and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eleazar-ben-simon">Eleazar ben Simon</a>. The survivors escaped to the foot of the pass under cover of darkness, leaving 5,300 infantry and 480 cavalry dead. Fergus Millar called the defeat a rare case of Roman regulars beaten decisively in a province by its own population.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The rebels pursued the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> to Antipatris and seized their artillery and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battering-ram">battering rams</a>. Gallus died soon afterwards, possibly by his own hand, and Mucianus succeeded him. Many more volunteers and towns joined the revolt; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eleazar-ben-simon">Eleazar ben Simon</a>, Joshua ben Zafia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a> the Perean became regional governors of the new Judean government, while Simon Bar Giora was driven from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> and raised his own army in the south. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nero">Nero</a> and the senate then sent Vespasian with four legions to crush the rebellion.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Beth Horon (66)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After rebels routed the Syrian governor Cestius Gallus at <b>Beth Horon</b>, an assembly in Jerusalem appointed the former high priest Ananus ben Ananus and Joseph ben Gurion to lead it and sent commanders to the districts, Josephus among them to Galilee.</blockquote><a href="/state/provisional-government-of-judea">Provisional Government of Judea</a></li></ul></section>
